From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 23:14:07 +0200
Subject: media: sun6i-isp: Copy only the white balance records the hardware
 writes

The white balance region is allocated 0x1000, which would be 256 window
records, and the whole of it was handed to userspace. The hardware only
writes the first 0x200. The remaining 224 records were whatever the
buffer held before, so a reader that summed the array got a number with
no relation to the frame: 1869915597 pixels for a 1920000 pixel frame,
which is what made the block look broken.

It is not broken. Measured on a solid white field through the raw bayer
path, every record from 0 to 31 satisfies sum == 254 * count on all
three channels, and no record from 32 on does, at every window geometry
tried. Copy the 0x200 the hardware fills and leave the rest of the
member as the zeros it was already memset to, which is what the vendor's
own "discard a window whose count >> 3 is zero" test expects.

The populated records form a grid eight columns wide whose cell tracks
the programmed window exactly up to 384 pixels of width and stops doing
so above it; that, and the four rows that are missing from what the cell
height implies, are recorded in the documentation rather than guessed at
here.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
---
 drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_stats.c       | 13 +++++++++-
 drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/uapi/sun6i-isp-config.h | 11 ++++++--
 2 files changed, 21 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_stats.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_stats.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_stats.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/sun6i_isp_stats.c
@@ -33,6 +33,17 @@
 #define SUN6I_ISP_STATS_AFS_OFFSET	0xf00
 #define SUN6I_ISP_STATS_AWB_OFFSET	0x1100
 
+/*
+ * The white balance region is allocated 0x1000, which would be 256 window
+ * records, but the hardware only ever writes the first 0x200 of it. Copying
+ * the whole region hands userspace 224 records of whatever the buffer held
+ * before, and a reader that sums the array gets a number with no relation to
+ * the frame. Measured on a solid white field: records 0 to 31 all satisfy
+ * sum == 254 * count, and record 32 onwards does not, at every window
+ * geometry tried.
+ */
+#define SUN6I_ISP_STATS_AWB_SIZE	0x200
+
 /* Stats */
 
 static void sun6i_isp_stats_buffer_fill(struct sun6i_isp_device *isp_dev,
@@ -74,7 +85,7 @@ static void sun6i_isp_stats_buffer_fill(struct sun6i_isp_device *isp_dev,
 
 	if (modules & SUN6I_ISP_MODULE_EN_AWB) {
 		memcpy(&stats->awb, base + SUN6I_ISP_STATS_AWB_OFFSET,
-		       sizeof(stats->awb));
+		       SUN6I_ISP_STATS_AWB_SIZE);
 		blocks |= SUN6I_ISP_STAT_AWB;
 	}
 
diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/uapi/sun6i-isp-config.h b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/uapi/sun6i-isp-config.h
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/uapi/sun6i-isp-config.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/sun6i-isp/uapi/sun6i-isp-config.h
@@ -302,8 +302,15 @@ struct sun6i_isp_stats_afs {
 /*
  * Per-window channel sums and the pixel count they cover. The vendor
  * discards a window whose count is under 8 and otherwise normalises by
- * count >> 3. Up to a 16 by 16 grid, with the origin depending on the
- * bayer phase.
+ * count >> 3. The origin depends on the bayer phase.
+ *
+ * The array is sized for the region the hardware is given, but only the
+ * first 32 records are populated on the A83T; the rest are zeroed, so
+ * the vendor's own count test rejects them. The populated records form
+ * a grid eight columns wide whose cell size follows the programmed
+ * window, exactly, up to a window 384 pixels wide -- beyond that the
+ * relation breaks down and is not yet understood, so treat a wide
+ * window's numbers as unreliable rather than as a measurement.
  */
 struct sun6i_isp_stats_awb_window {
 	__u32	sum[3];
-- 
